WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.140
- On the screen I have these four shapes,

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:03.630
and I'll give you a quick story about

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.170
each one of these shapes very quickly.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.120
And then I'll explain an
aspect of machine learning

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.470
behind these shapes.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.950
The first shape is, very simply a line.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:16.030
So, for example, imagine
depressingly somewhat,

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.160
if you're trying to buy
a house in the Bay Area,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.070
and you make a plot of
the price of the house

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.930
versus the square footage.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.860
You will see a linear relationship

00:00:26.562 --> 00:00:30.160
and in machine learning this
is called a regression problem.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.370
Where imagine that somebody said

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:32.910
I want you to predict the house price,

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.410
I'll give you the square footage,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.620
you might draw a line
through the set of points

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.823
that you've collected.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.460
And that might give you some sense

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.480
for how much you'll end
up paying for the house.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.840
And obviously you'll decide
you can't pay for it.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.240
But, nonetheless, that's the
process you'll go through.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.790
So, I want to give you some insight

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.930
into how does this machine
learning process work?

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.530
What does regression do?

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.410
Regression assumes that
your data looks like

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.340
a line or a plane.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.890
And then it tries to find the best

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.780
line or plane that looks like your data.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.580
Okay, so keep that verbiage in mind.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.950
Another common problem that
we solve in many different

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.960
contexts in business or in science,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.410
is called clustering.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.110
We're given a bunch of data

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.620
and you're trying to determine

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.470
are there self-similar parts of the data?

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.400
So for example if you've
collected data about

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.560
let's say, a lot of
patients who have diabetes,

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.050
Type II diabetes, let's say,

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.670
you might want to know, are
all Type II diabetes patients

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.930
similar or are there multiple types

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.660
of Type II diabetes patients.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.000
So you might attempt to solve
it via clustering problem.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.410
And the way clustering works,

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.050
is again very similar to regression,

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.240
in that you assume that
your data breaks up

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.760
into multiple pieces.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.290
And then you try and find
the best decomposition

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.520
of your data into that
many number of pieces.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.640
Right, so, similar to regression,

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.280
you assume a particular shape,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.760
and then you try and look
for that shape in your data.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.050
The third example is a loop.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.170
Imagine that your are an economist

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.050
and for every year you figure out

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:06.620
the interest rate of the Fed

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.560
and the growth rate of the economy.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.680
And these are your two
axes and you put a dot

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.900
for every year that you collect the data.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.210
You will notice that the data evolves

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.420
in these cyclic patterns

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.830
where when the interest
rates are low, economies grow

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.460
and then eventually when the
economies have grown too much,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.030
the interest rates are tightened

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.930
and the economy start to shrink again.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.830
Again we are in Topsy
Turvy world these days,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.693
so no bearing to real world,
these days, apparently.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.970
But nonetheless, that's what
you would see in your data.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.520
I'll come back to that
picture in a second.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.050
Look at the fourth picture.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.170
Imagine that you are, let's say,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.160
flying from San Francisco to New York,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.920
and you stick two sensors on the plane.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.426
You measure the amount
of lift that the plane

00:02:49.426 --> 00:02:52.600
is experiencing versus the drag

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.260
that the plane is experiencing.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.550
And when you plot the
picture on the screen,

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.460
you'll see a picture
like this fourth picture

00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.720
on the screen here.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.870
Which is that, for the most of the flight

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.670
you will be in the middle
part of the region,

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.600
where lift and drag change a
little but not a whole lot.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.680
But there's a few anomalous
things that happen

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.690
you take off at some point,
you land at some point,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.940
and you might go into turbulence

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.270
and come back at some point,

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:19.290
and those are the three flares
that you see here, okay.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.000
Now with this background,
I want to make two points.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.530
The first point is that
the shape of the data

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.960
actually contains a lot
of information about it.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.660
If you knew the shape of the data,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.890
you wouldn't have to ask many
questions about it going in.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.910
The second is we have great algorithms

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:39.510
called Regression and Clustering

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.530
that solve the first two problems,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.490
but we don't have an algorithm called

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.690
loop detect or flare detect.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.500
And in fact, the category of shapes

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.040
that you actually see
exhibited in real data sets

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.860
is so vast, that it's kind of pointless

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.440
to come up with a prototypical shape first

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.890
and then look for the shape in the data.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:02.730
And so that is the motivation
behind using topology

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.550
to understand the shape of the data,

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:07.330
because the shape of
the data is meaningful.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.260
Everybody with me this far?

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:10.093
Yeah?

00:04:10.093 --> 00:04:11.320
All right then.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:16.000
So, for people,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.550
I'll go through this slide very quickly,

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.280
this if for people who
might be slightly more

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.313
technical in the machine learning space.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.390
But I just wanted to give you a sense

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.700
of sort of how this interacts
with machine learning,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.990
and how it's dependent
on machine learning.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:31.920
So, what you see in this picture,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.400
there are three datasets,

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.140
every row represents a dataset.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:39.443
So the first dataset are
these two clasping points,

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.160
half the points are blue
and the other half are red.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.560
The shape in the middle has
blue points at the middle

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:47.980
and then there's the circle that

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.950
of red points that envelops that.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.740
And then the third shape
is basically two clusters,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.440
red points here and blue points here.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:56.163
Kind of like politics, I guess.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.570
And what you see in
every column thereafter

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:03.350
is a particular machine learning algorithm

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:06.080
trying to discriminate
between the blue points

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.730
and the red points, okay.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.230
So you see that decision surface

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:11.370
of these machine learning algorithms.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.760
The exact names of these
algorithms aren't important,

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.850
so don't worry about those.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.320
What's important here is
that you can see two things,

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.730
the first thing is that data has shape,

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:24.500
as you can see immediately
in this, for example.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.910
The second thing you can also see is that

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.600
every algorithm also has a shape.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.200
So for example, if you
look at the second column,

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.360
which is the first colored
sort of picture with the bands,

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.260
you will notice that no matter

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.240
what the shape of the data is,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.510
the algorithm always has
this linear structure

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:42.970
where it will find a line

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.740
and then it will
discriminate away from it.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.010
If you look at the second
from the last column,

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.310
you will see that this
algorithm has a shape,

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:55.480
which is that it wants to
discriminate data into boxes.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.410
So it wants to find axis aligned boxes

00:05:58.410 --> 00:05:59.913
that have various opacities.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:04.010
And so the key realization here
is that data has shape

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:06.280
and algorithms also have shape.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.210
The algorithms that we use
currently in machine learning.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.620
So it is really important for us,

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.220
if you are going to hope to understand

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.630
what's going on in these algorithms

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:16.220
and how they make decisions,

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.240
it's really important for us to understand

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.890
how these two things are linked together.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:24.140
How algorithms work together
with the shape of the data

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.080
and when will they make errors,

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.750
and what kind of errors with they make?

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.680
Everybody with me this far?

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.270
So just quick summary

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:33.500
and then I'll switch into examples

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.550
which will be a lot more fun.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:40.270
Topological data analysis
basically is a formalization

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:42.630
using this old area of
math called topology

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:44.110
that allows us to study shape,

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.240
shape of data, shape of algorithms,

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.450
shape of decision surfaces.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.600
For people who are slightly
more technical in the audience,

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.480
it allows us to merge together the results

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.240
of various other algorithms
in a coherent way

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.360
that you can learn from it together.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.370
And the final point, the
most interesting point

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.200
is that it's a compressed representation,

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.040
it compresses the data.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.840
And compression is really important

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.470
because when people
talk about big datasets,

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.240
how vast it is, big datasets are actually,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.300
kind of useless.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.610
You want datasets that
are as small as possible.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:20.100
You want to eliminate complexity
that's meaningless, right?

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.810
So, if you go back to the thing
that I said about the map,

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.460
there's a lot of complexity in that map,

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.510
the exact shape of the
coast is kind of irrelevant.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.340
So there are many, many details
in data that are irrelevant

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.430
and it's actually very
useful to understand

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.400
the essential complexity of the data.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.100
So compression is a very key part

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.840
of all sorts of machine learning

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.030
and in particular,
topological data analysis

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:44.400
is kind of based on outputting
a compressed representation.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.090
And it also happens to a visualize-able

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:49.094
which is very, very, very useful.

